@@s001 HOG: histograms of gradients
@@s002 dato:gradient of a pixel,ora:pixel that votes,ok:histogram bar
@@s003 A cell with four pixels where the gradient is not zero (simplified example, 4 direction intervals). The arrow shows the direction of the gradient, its length the magnitude.
@@s004 First pixel: direction 0°, magnitude 2. It votes in the 0° interval with weight <b>2</b>.
@@s005 Second: 0°, magnitude 1. The 0° interval reaches <b>3</b>.
@@s006 Third: 90°, magnitude 3.
@@s007 Fourth: 45°, magnitude 1. The pixels without gradient (all the others) vote with weight 0.
@@s008 The histogram of the cell is [3, 1, 3, 0]. In the blocks it is normalised by dividing by the norm √19 ≈ 4.36: [0.69, 0.23, 0.69, 0]. What counts is the shape, not the contrast.
@@s009 dato:pixel,ok:gradient (differences),no:changed
@@s010 HOG: histograms of gradients
@@s011 Lecture 9 and the exercises of week 11 (<i>HoG</i>, <i>HoG classifier</i>). HOG (Histogram of Oriented Gradients) turns an image into a vector of features that describes the <b>shape</b>: the directions of the edges, cell by cell. It combines the <a href="w08-derivate-bordi.html">gradients</a> of week 8 and the classifiers of week 10. Exam questions <b>Q6 (b)</b> and <b>Q8 (c)</b>.
@@s012 How it is built
@@s013 From the image to the vector
@@s014 With derivative filters (like Sobel): where there is an edge the magnitude is large, the direction is perpendicular to the edge.
@@s015 The directions are divided into a few intervals (in the exercise <code>orientations=8</code>); each pixel votes for its interval with a weight equal to the magnitude of the gradient. Flat pixels vote almost zero.
@@s016 Each block is divided by its norm: so brightness and contrast matter little, the shape matters. The blocks overlap.
@@s017 Image 64 × 64: 8 × 8 cells, 7 × 7 = 49 blocks, each 2 × 2 × 8 = 32 numbers ⇒ <b>1568</b> numbers. Image 128 × 64: <b>3360</b>. Verified with <code>skimage.feature.hog</code>.
@@s018 Check: in an image with a single vertical edge (black on the left, white on the right), the cells next to the edge put all the weight in one interval, that of the horizontal direction of the gradient.
@@s019 The pixels vote: the histogram of a cell
@@s020 In the exercise
@@s021 The three parameters of steps 2–3. <code>visualize=True</code> also returns the image with the "little stars" of the dominant directions; <code>channel_axis=-1</code> says that the last axis is the colours.
@@s022 One HOG vector per image: the data matrix (images × features) on which a linear classifier or an SVM is trained. With a <b>sliding window</b> (Task 3 of the classifier) the same model is applied to every piece of a large image.
@@s023 Why it works better than the pixels
@@s024 <b>Light</b>: the gradients look at differences, not absolute values, and the block normalisation removes the contrast.
@@s025 <b>Small shifts</b>: inside a cell of 8 × 8 what counts is how many directions there are, not exactly where.
@@s026 <b>Shape</b>: a standing person has many vertical edges (legs, torso) in similar positions: the HOG vector captures it.
@@s027 Limits: large rotations and changes of scale change the cells; the parameters (cells, intervals) are chosen by hand. Convolutional networks learn similar filters by themselves (week 14).
@@s028 Better features, simpler classifier
@@s029 Review question of week 9: why does choosing good features often matter more than the classifier? With HOG, classes that in pixel space are intertwined become almost separable by a hyperplane: a linear classifier is enough. It is the same idea as the polar coordinates for the <a href="w10-confini-non-lineari.html">ring</a>.
@@s030 Q6 (b) and Q8 (c)
@@s031 Q6 (b): from derivatives and gradients to HOG (steps 1–4). Q8 (c): HOG as features for a linear classifier or an SVM, with the sliding window.
@@s032 Light, shifts, shape
